<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Technology in Education – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.2em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Technology in Education</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> When It's Better to Ignore Technology</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Interpret spoken advice on common problems. Interactive: Respond to questions in group discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can interpret spoken advice to understand limits on technology use (e.g., "I think this app is helpful because it makes it easy to access our homework and assignments, but I try not to use it too much so I don't get distracted by my phone."). Can respond to questions in group discussions by sharing opinions on technology use (e.g., "I love this app because it has fun videos."). Can respond in discussions by giving examples of useful or distracting apps (e.g., "In the audio, it is clear that video apps distract students during class.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a series of images showing people using technology (e.g., studying with a tablet, texting at dinner, playing games in class) and people *not* using technology (e.g., reading a physical book, talking face-to-face, enjoying nature). Students observe the images. The teacher asks, "Look at these pictures. When do you think using technology is *beneficial*, and when do you think it's *better to ignore* it? Why?" Students share quick initial thoughts in English.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to express opinions and suggestions using the target grammar. "I think using *apps* for schoolwork is *useful*, but we *should* sometimes put our *devices* away." The teacher pronounces key vocabulary (e.g., "technology," "benefit," "problem," "solution," "ignore") clearly, focusing on stress in multisyllabic words like "education" and "innovation." The teacher also models the intonation patterns for modal verbs "should" and "can" in suggestions and abilities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of new concepts and vocabulary.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If something is 'beneficial,' is it good or bad?" (Good)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does it mean to 'ignore' technology? Does it mean to use it?" (No, it means to not use it, to pay no attention to it.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can we always use technology, or *should* we sometimes *limit* it?" (We should sometimes limit it.)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Technology in Education".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio recording (e.g., a short podcast segment or an interview clip) of two teenagers discussing "When It's Better to Ignore Technology." The audio will feature opinions on technology use in school, common problems, and suggestions for limiting device usage. It incorporates simple present for opinions, modal "should" for suggestions, and target vocabulary (technology, apps, benefit, problem, solution, device, use, ignore, prefer, advise, limit, appropriate, useful, while, but, instead, because).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Can interpret spoken advice on common problems and respond to questions in group discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First Listening (Gist):</b> The teacher plays the audio once. Students listen and answer: "What is the main topic of their conversation?" and "Do they think technology is *always* good, or does it have *problems*?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second Listening (True/False):</b> The teacher provides a handout with 3-4 True/False statements about the audio content. Students listen again and mark T or F. For example:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The teenagers think technology is always useful for studying." (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One suggestion is to ignore phones during lunch." (True)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Third Listening (Information Matching):</b> The teacher provides a list of common problems (e.g., "distraction in class," "less face-to-face talk") and suggested solutions (e.g., "tech-free times," "turn off notifications"). Students listen to the audio one more time and match the problem to the solution mentioned by the speakers.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific details, interpretations of advice, and responses in discussions. Grammar: Simple present for expressing opinions and comparing preferences, modal "should" for suggestions, modal "can" for ability, Wh-questions. Vocabulary: technology, apps, tools, school, education, benefit, problem, solution, device, use, ignore, prefer, advise, limit, appropriate, useful, beneficial, while, but, instead, because. Pronunciation: Stress in multisyllabic words (e.g., "education," "innovation"), minimal pairs /ʊ/ in "should" and /uː/ in "shooed," intonation patterns with modal verbs.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listening for Gaps):</b> The teacher distributes a transcript of selected sentences from the audio with key vocabulary words (e.g., "problem," "solution," "apps," "limit," "benefit") or grammatical structures (e.g., "should," "can," simple present verbs) omitted. Students listen to the audio segment again and fill in the blanks.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "I think this _____ is helpful ________ it makes it easy to _______ our homework, but I try not to use it too much so I don't get distracted by my phone." (app, because, access)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "You _______ ignore technology when you are eating with your family." (should)</li>
                        </ul>
                        Teacher plays the audio segments twice, pausing as needed. Teacher monitors for correct listening comprehension of target vocabulary and grammar.
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Listening for Advice and Rephrasing):</b> The teacher provides a list of common "problems" related to technology in school. Students listen to specific segments of the original audio again. For each problem, students identify the *advice* or *solution* given by the speakers in the audio, formulating a sentence using "should."
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher provides: "Problem: Students get distracted by their phones during class."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen and rephrase the audio's advice: "We *should* turn off notifications while in class." or "Students *should* *limit* their phone *use* in class."</li>
                        </ul>
                        The teacher guides students to listen for the specific modal structures and vocabulary.
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Listening to Peer Interpretation):</b> In pairs, students discuss two questions based on the audio. They listen carefully to their partner's interpretation of the audio.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"According to the audio, what is one *benefit* of using technology in school, and what is one *problem*?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What advice did you hear about when it's *better to ignore* technology?"</li>
                        </ul>
                        The teacher circulates, ensures students are actively listening to each other's responses, and prompts them to use the target vocabulary and grammar while discussing the audio's content. Focus on correct pronunciation of "should" and "can" and stress in multi-syllabic words.
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Technology in Education."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small group discussion (3-4 students).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students form small groups. The teacher presents a few discussion questions on the board that require students to connect the audio's advice to their personal experiences and opinions. The primary listening focus for students is to actively listen to each group member's response and opinion.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Discussion Questions:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What *apps* do you use for school? Do you think they are *useful* or *distracting*? Why?" (Grammar: Simple present, Wh-questions. Vocab: apps, useful, distracting, because)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"From the audio, one speaker said we *should* ignore *devices* when eating. Do you agree? Why or why not?" (Grammar: Simple present, should. Vocab: ignore, devices, benefit, problem)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one *solution* your group can think of to *limit* the *problem* of too much screen time in school?" (Grammar: can, Wh-questions. Vocab: solution, limit, problem, school)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student takes turns sharing their opinion and listening carefully to their group members. They *respond to questions* and *give examples*. The teacher circulates, listens to discussions, prompts students to elaborate, and encourages the use of target vocabulary and grammatical structures. Pronunciation of stress and intonation patterns should be monitored.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> In their groups (or individually if preferred), students quickly summarize one piece of advice from the audio they found most important and briefly explain why, or identify a specific problem mentioned in the audio and share a suggestion for a *solution*. They must use target vocabulary and grammar to express their interpretation and opinion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly interprets spoken advice/problems from the audio.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds to questions intelligibly and clearly states opinions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., technology, benefit, problem, advise, limit) appropriately.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses simple present for opinions and "should" for suggestions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates appropriate stress in multisyllabic words and intonation patterns with modals.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students write their answers to the following questions on a small slip of paper before leaving class:
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most important advice you heard today about when it's *better to ignore* technology?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new *vocabulary word* or *phrase* you learned today related to technology?"</li>
                </ol></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well did you understand the different pieces of advice given in the audio today about technology use?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How easy or difficult was it to respond to your classmates' opinions about technology use during the group discussion?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the listening activities worked well? Did students understand the main points and specific details of the audio? Was the pacing appropriate for the different listening tasks?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled to interpret spoken advice or engage in the group discussion? (e.g., provide more visual aids, pre-teach more vocabulary, re-play audio segments multiple times, or offer more structured sentence starters for discussions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Was the target vocabulary and grammar effectively integrated and practiced during the listening activities?</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "Great job listening and discussing today! In our next lesson (Lesson 2 of 5), we will continue exploring our theme, 'When It's Better to Ignore Technology,' but we will *read* a short article that uses many of the same vocabulary words we heard today. This will help us understand more about the *benefits* and *problems* of *technology* in *education* through a different skill."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>